\title{\textbf{Coordination-Collapse Phase Boundary in Policy-Gradient Cooperative MARL}}

\author{Anonymous Authors\\ Paper under double-blind review}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
Cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) is known to collapse
into a degenerate \emph{lazy-agent} policy when reward centralization falls
too low, but this failure has never been characterized as a quantitative
object. We treat it as one: we measure the critical centralization $\wcrit$
at which coordination gives way to collapse---with a pre-registered collapse
definition and bootstrap confidence intervals---turning a qualitative failure
mode into a measurable phase boundary, the first in deep cooperative MARL.
The boundary is an instrument, and our central finding uses it to characterize
how \emph{loss aversion} governs coordination: loss-averse agents
($\lambda>1$) systematically \emph{raise} $\wcrit$---they over-weight the
immediate local cost of cooperating against its diffuse, delayed global benefit,
so the team needs more reward centralization to coordinate. This is our most
strongly supported result: the rightward shift is monotone in $\lambda$,
saturating, and \emph{replicates across four environment--algorithm settings}:
the supply chain under PPO and under centralized-critic MAPPO, MPE Cooperative
Navigation under MAPPO, and Level-Based Foraging under MAPPO---i.e.\ three
environments and two algorithms (four of the six possible combinations). On the supply chain a full sweep
$\lambda{=}1{\ldots}7$ is tracked by a one-parameter, theory-bounded law (RMSE
$0.014$): the shift is $+0.089$ $[0.041, 0.141]$ at $\lambda{=}3$ (within the
human range) and up to $+0.166$ $[0.135, 0.202]$ at $\lambda{=}7$ (a high-stakes
upper bound). A mean-field signal-to-noise account \emph{fixes this sign a priori}
(proved as a sign invariant of the escape barrier) and is independently
corroborated by an exploration-noise ablation. Two further results sharpen the
picture but are scoped to the supply-chain substrate, where they hold cleanly. A
three-way decomposition shows probability-weighting curvature moving $\wcrit$ in
the \emph{opposite} direction (left by $0.100$ $[0.033, 0.172]$); and---a
striking, falsifiable prediction we establish on this one substrate---the
boundary is set by a \emph{minority by network position}: a single
\emph{downstream} loss-averse node drives $\wcrit=0.853$, indistinguishable from
a fully loss-averse population ($0.851$), whereas the same bias placed upstream
moves it far less ($0.726$). Where a directed resource-flow ordering exists, this
predicts a system's centralization requirement is fixed by its most downstream
biased participant, not the mean. The boundary doubles as a safety margin: how
much more centralization a cooperative system needs once its agents are
loss-averse.
\end{abstract}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

% ---- Opening: motivating question and mental model ---------------------
% TODO-CITE: add a human--AI teaming / hybrid-intelligence reference on this
% opening sentence (no suitable key currently in references_aaai.bib).
Cooperative systems increasingly mix human decision-makers with learning agents
trained to act like them. In automated replenishment, fraud review, energy
dispatch, and clinical triage, the deployed agents do not merely optimise a
clean objective---they inherit human preference structure by design, either
because they are trained on human choices through preference-aligned objectives
that are themselves prospect-theoretic in form~\citep{ethayarajh2024kto}, or
because they operate alongside humans in the loop~\citep{lalmohammed2025}. The
most robustly documented feature of that preference structure is \emph{loss
aversion}: people---and now the agents shaped after them---weigh a loss more
heavily than an equivalent gain~\citep{kahneman1979,tversky1992}.

This raises a system-level question that individual-level behavioral economics
does not answer. We understand what loss aversion does to a \emph{single}
decision-maker; we do not understand what it does to the \emph{coordination} of
many decision-makers learning together~\citep{leibo2017multi,lowe2017multi}. The mental model we formalise is
concrete: a team of loss-averse participants managing a shared pipeline, each
acting on its own incentives, jointly responsible for an outcome none of them
controls alone. The real-world ability we want is to say, \emph{before}
deployment, how strongly such a system must be incentivised toward the
collective good to keep it from quietly falling apart---and whether one biased
participant in the wrong place can drag the whole system down. Whether that
ability can be provided at all is the question; we show that it can, by turning
a known-but-vague failure mode into a measured object.

% ---- I3 folded in: the failure mode, never located ---------------------
\subsection{Coordination collapse as an unmeasured boundary}
Cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) has a well-documented
failure mode---\emph{coordination collapse}, in which agents abandon cooperation
for a degenerate, zero-action \emph{lazy-agent}
policy~\citep{liu2023lazy,peng2023curo}. Whether a system collapses depends on
its \emph{reward centralization} $w\in[0,1]$, the fraction of each agent's reward
drawn from the team's global outcome rather than its own local one: push $w$ too
low and each agent's own performance drowns out its contribution to the team, so
cooperation carries no learnable signal and the safe, lazy policy wins. But the
field describes this only qualitatively---``centralization
helps''~\citep{liu2023lazy,dmarl2025}. \emph{Where}, in $w$, coordination gives
way to collapse---the \emph{critical centralization} $\wcrit$, the value of $w$
at which the probability of collapse crosses $0.5$---has never been measured.
\citet{liu2023lazy} and \citet{peng2023curo} formalise the lazy-agent problem and
relative overgeneralization and propose mitigations; \citet{weng2026} identify
representation degradation under decentralised exploration; \citet{dmarl2025}
show decentralised reward shaping cannot close the gap to centralised baselines.
All treat the boundary as a qualitative fact, not a quantity to estimate. The
nearest attempts sweep adjacent axes without isolating a critical
value---\citet{wang2025maca} optimise win rates over credit-assignment levels,
\citet{jiang2018dgn} tune reward-mixing weights---and the closest in spirit,
\citet{yamaguchi2025}, builds a phase diagram for independent Q-learning over
\emph{environmental} axes (grid size, agent density), holding fixed precisely the
reward structure $w$ and behavioral profile we vary. No prior work characterises
the collapse boundary as a quantitative object with confidence intervals.

% ---- I4 folded in: behavioral MARL is entangled ------------------------
\subsection{Why prospect theory and MARL have not yet met cleanly}
A parallel literature equips MARL agents with risk preferences but cannot answer
our question. Risk-sensitive methods based on Conditional Value at Risk optimise
tail outcomes~\citep{qiu2021rmix,xie2023riskq}---mathematically distinct from
loss aversion, an asymmetry of perceived value around a reference point rather
than a reweighting of tail probability. \citet{lalmohammed2025} bring cumulative
prospect theory (CPT) into cooperative MARL but fold the value function and the
probability-weighting function jointly into the actor--critic update, so the
effect of curvature ($\alpha,\beta$) and of loss aversion ($\lambda$) on
coordination cannot be told apart. In evolutionary game theory,
\citet{mengesha2025} prove loss aversion induces population-level
lock-in---establishing \emph{conceptually} that behavioral bias can move a
coordination tipping point, but in a world of fixed strategies under replicator
selection, with no learned policies, function approximation, or finite-horizon
training dynamics. Two questions thus sit open and entangled: coordination
collapse has never been measured as a boundary, and behavioral bias has never
been decomposed \emph{against} such a boundary to say which component moves it,
and in which direction.

% ---- I5 folded in: our approach and why it is right --------------------
\subsection{Approach: the boundary as an instrument}
We close both halves at once, and they discipline each other---a behavioral
shift is only interpretable against a quantified null boundary, and a boundary is
only worth quantifying if something scientifically meaningful moves it. We treat
collapse the way physics treats a phase boundary: a pre-registered, frozen binary
collapse definition; logistic estimation of $P(\mathrm{collapse}\mid w)$; and
bootstrap confidence intervals on the critical centralization $\wcrit$. This
yields, to our knowledge, the first quantitative phase boundary of coordination
collapse in deep cooperative MARL. We use ``phase boundary'' in the empirical
sense---a sharp, metastable threshold whose asymptotic ($T\!\to\!\infty$) value
is the boundary proper---not as a claim of a thermodynamic-limit singularity.
The boundary is then an \emph{instrument}, and two design choices make what it
measures trustworthy. We apply CPT as a \emph{reward transform} after reward
blending rather than modifying the learning update, so the optimiser is identical
across conditions and a measured shift cannot be confounded with an algorithmic
one; and we estimate the boundary at multiple training horizons, because a
threshold that drifts with training time would otherwise masquerade as a fixed
constant. Against this null, a three-way design (no PT / curvature only /
curvature plus loss aversion) attributes any shift in $\wcrit$ to a specific CPT
component.

% ---- I2 folded in: why this domain -------------------------------------
\subsection{Why the multi-echelon supply chain is the right substrate}
We ground the measurement in automated replenishment for multi-echelon supply
chains, a canonical cooperative MARL problem in which agents coordinate ordering
across a network under delayed feedback, partial observability, and structurally
misaligned local incentives~\citep{oroojlooyjadid2022,gijsbrechts2022,hubbs2020orgym,marlim2023}. The
domain is not incidental, for three reasons. Its failure modes are
\emph{economically legible}: agents that hoard, under-order, or stop ordering
produce the bullwhip effect, stockouts, and cascading losses, so collapse here is
a recognisable operational disaster, not an abstract training pathology. It
supplies the \emph{mental model} directly: real replenishment is done by planners
who demonstrably weigh losses more heavily than gains, and the agents replacing
or assisting them inherit that bias by design. And it has a \emph{directed
flow}---inventory moves downstream along a chain, so ``upstream'' and
``downstream'' are well-defined---which lets us ask not just whether bias matters
but \emph{where in the network} a biased participant matters most, the question
the project was conceived to answer.

% ---- I6: core research question + updated RQs --------------------------
\subsection{Research questions}
Our core question is: \emph{does behavioral loss aversion move the coordination
boundary of a cooperative learning system, in a direction we can predict and a
magnitude we can measure---and is that movement set by the population or by a
minority?} We make it precise through four questions:
\textbf{(RQ1, Boundary)} does collapse occur at a sharp $\wcrit$---a phase
boundary, not gradual decay---and is $\wcrit$ constant or does it slide with the
training horizon $T$? \textbf{(RQ2, Decomposition)} how do the two prospect-theory
components, curvature ($\alpha,\beta$) and loss aversion ($\lambda$), each shift
$\wcrit$, and in which directions? \textbf{(RQ3, Mechanism and generality)} is the
loss-aversion shift a substrate artifact, or does a mechanism fix its \emph{sign}
a priori and the shift replicate across environments and algorithms?
\textbf{(RQ4, Locus)} is the boundary a population-wide average, or can a
\emph{minority at a particular network position} set it for the whole system?

% ---- I7: findings -------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Findings and contributions}
Our results answer all four questions. \emph{(RQ1)} Across 1{,}524 controlled
runs, the boundary is sharp---a logistic phase transition in $w$, not a gradual
degradation---and it is not a fixed constant: it slides left with the training
horizon
($\wcrit = 0.822\!\to\!0.666$ from 150k to 300k steps), so collapse at
intermediate $w$ is a \emph{metastable} attractor rather than an absorbing
state. Against this null boundary, \emph{(RQ2)} \emph{loss aversion} is our
central and most strongly supported behavioral effect: it shifts $\wcrit$
\emph{right}, \emph{impeding} coordination---a loss-averse agent over-weights the
immediate local cost of cooperating against its diffuse, delayed global benefit.
The shift is $+0.089$ $[0.041, 0.141]$ at $\lambda{=}3$
(near the canonical human estimate $\lambda\approx2.25$) and grows to
$+0.166$ $[0.135, 0.202]$ at $\lambda{=}7$ (a high-stakes upper bound); a full
sweep $\lambda{=}1{\ldots}7$ rises then saturates, tracked by a one-parameter,
theory-bounded affine law (RMSE $0.014$; non-overlapping $\lambda{=}1$ vs
$\lambda{=}7$ intervals). \emph{(RQ3)} Crucially, this is not a substrate
artifact. A mean-field signal-to-noise account fixes the \emph{sign} before any
data are seen---we prove it as a sign invariant of the escape barrier
(\Cref{thm:sign}), and an exploration-noise ablation corroborates the same
mechanism---and the shift itself replicates across two further
environments (MPE Cooperative Navigation, $\wcrit$ rising $0.78\!\to\!0.89$; and
Level-Based Foraging, $0.26\!\to\!0.83$) under
centralized-critic MAPPO, and under a different algorithm on the supply
chain (MAPPO, $0.80\!\to\!0.89$; \Cref{fig:generalization}). All \emph{four
environment--algorithm settings}---three environments under two algorithms---show
the same direction: this is the generalizing core of the paper.

Two further results sharpen the picture but are established on the supply-chain
substrate alone, where its continuous, per-agent economic reward makes them
finely identifiable. First, a three-way decomposition shows
probability-weighting curvature moving $\wcrit$ in the \emph{opposite} direction
(\emph{left} by $0.100$ $[0.033, 0.172]$, \emph{easing} coordination)---a
substrate-specific effect we report but, unlike the loss-aversion direction, do
not claim generalizes. Second, \emph{(RQ4)} the boundary is set not by the
average bias but by a \emph{minority by network position}: a single
\emph{downstream} loss-averse node moves the system boundary to
$\wcrit = 0.853$---indistinguishable from a fully loss-averse population
($0.851$)---while an upstream node shifts it far less ($0.726$), a cascade that
propagates upstream along the ordering chain (App.~\ref{sec:appendix_heterogeneous}).
We present this as a striking but single-substrate result, and frame it as a
\emph{falsifiable, structurally specified} prediction (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}):
in any cooperative domain with a directed resource-flow ordering, a downstream
loss-averse minority should set the boundary.

To our knowledge this is the first quantitative, confidence-intervalled phase
boundary of coordination collapse in deep cooperative MARL, and the first to show
that loss aversion shifts it. The delta is precise: \citet{mengesha2025} establish
that loss aversion can move a coordination tipping point, but in evolutionary game
theory over fixed strategies; \citet{lalmohammed2025} bring CPT into cooperative
MARL but without separating its components or measuring a boundary; prior
phase-structure work~\citep{yamaguchi2025,capannoli2026phase} charts environmental
axes for tabular learners or fixed update rules. Our contribution is the
\emph{measured, CI'd boundary} and the \emph{component decomposition} against it,
in gradient-trained neural policies---a measurable safety margin for how much
centralization a cooperative system needs, and how much \emph{more} once its
agents are loss-averse (\Cref{fig:heatmap},~\Cref{fig:decomposition}).

\begin{figure}[tbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{../docs/expT_figures/collapse_heatmap.png}
  \caption{%
