monotonic $\wcrit(\lambda)$; \emph{affine} form of $\phi(\lambda)$ with
slope $\rho\in[0,1]$; abrupt (phase-transition) sharpness; $\wcrit$
decreasing with horizon $T$; $\wcrit$ increasing with exploration noise. \\
\midrule
\textbf{Empirically confirmed} & the opposite signs and affine form
(Section~\ref{sec:results_behavioral}); the horizon slide (Exp~A/T); and the
exploration-noise dependence---raising the entropy coefficient moves
$\wcrit$ right and collapses the system at large noise
(Section~\ref{sec:appendix_robustness2}). \\
\midrule
\textbf{Calibrated (1 param/axis)} & $g/c$ (from the $\lambda{=}1$
boundary); loss-domain share $\rho$; noise scale setting the absolute
$\wcrit$. \\
\midrule
\textbf{Not claimed} & a closed-form value of $\wcrit$ from the cost matrix;
the numerical magnitude on any axis; the mild concavity of $\wcrit(\lambda)$
beyond leading order. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scope.} The model is a representative-agent reduction with a
calibrated noise scale; $c$, $g$, $\sigma$ and $\rho$ are not derived from
the multi-echelon cost matrix. A direct finite-difference probe of the
substrate confirms the \emph{sign} of the local-cost/global-benefit
asymmetry but shows the marginal coefficients are operating-point dependent,
so the absolute boundary is a property of the stochastic multi-agent
dynamics rather than a static gradient---which is why we calibrate rather
than predict its magnitude. This section is not the paper's contribution---
the decomposition is---but a supporting, falsifiable \emph{mechanism}: it
commits in advance to the signs, the ordering, the affine $\lambda$-law with
a bounded slope, the sharpness, and the horizon and exploration-noise
directions (\Cref{tab:ledger}), all of which the data bear out, consistent
with exploration-driven accounts of cooperation failure in shared-policy
MARL~\citep{weng2026}. The exploration-noise prediction is confirmed
directly by the entropy ablation (Section~\ref{sec:appendix_robustness2}).
Section~\ref{sec:appendix_snr} reports a direct test that
measures the Langevin drift and diffusion from PPO training: it reproduces
the matched-horizon boundary but leaves the magnitude calibrated, as the
drift-sign observable is confounded by basin geometry.
We therefore claim this mechanism at the \emph{sign-and-form level only}---the
opposite signs, the monotone ordering, the affine form, and the horizon and
exploration-noise directions---and treat the numerical value of $\wcrit$ as
calibrated throughout. No result in this paper rests on a predicted magnitude;
upgrading the account from calibrated to predictive is left to future work.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Generalization across Environments and Algorithms}
\label{sec:generalization}

The mechanism of Section~\ref{sec:meanfield} commits, before any data, to a
\emph{direction}: loss aversion inflates gradient noise and therefore raises
$\wcrit$. If that account is right rather than an artefact of one substrate or
one optimiser, the sign should survive changing both. We test this on two
further environments and a second algorithm. The headline result is that the
\emph{direction} of the loss-aversion effect is robust to both, while the
absolute boundary, the curvature sign, and the precise functional form are
substrate- and algorithm-specific---exactly the split the calibrated-not-predicted
mechanism (\Cref{tab:ledger}) anticipates. \Cref{tab:claim_ledger}
(Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ledger}) records the breadth of evidence behind every
headline claim.
\Cref{fig:generalization} summarises the headline: in all four settings
$\wcrit$ climbs with $\lambda$, the curves converging in the mid-range and
differing most at $\lambda{=}1$.

\begin{figure}[tbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.82\linewidth]{../docs/generalization_figures/generalization_wcrit_vs_lambda.png}
  \caption{%
    \textbf{The loss-aversion effect generalizes.} Critical centralization
    $\wcrit$ versus loss aversion $\lambda$ for four settings: the supply chain
    under PPO (blue) and under centralized-critic MAPPO (green), MPE Cooperative
    Navigation under MAPPO (red), and Level-Based Foraging under MAPPO (purple).
    Error bars are bootstrap 95\% CIs. Across three structurally distinct
    environments and two algorithms, $\wcrit$ rises with $\lambda$ then
    saturates---loss aversion raises the centralization a cooperative system needs
    to avert collapse. The \emph{direction} is what generalizes; absolute levels
    differ (most at $\lambda{=}1$) and the curvature sign and exact functional
    form are substrate-specific. (LBF's high-$\lambda$ points saturate against the
    centralization ceiling and are reported as exploratory; see
    Section~\ref{sec:lbf}.)}
  \label{fig:generalization}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Across environments: incentive misalignment across domains}
\label{sec:appendix_environments}

A central claim of this work is that the coordination cliff---a sharp, CPT-modulated
collapse threshold as the reward centralization $w$ decreases---is a
\emph{recurring} feature of multi-agent systems where individual self-interest
conflicts with collective outcomes, not an artefact of the supply-chain setting.
We examine this claim in two qualitatively distinct environments from different
application domains---the supply chain (under PPO) and MPE Cooperative Navigation
(under centralised-critic MAPPO)---both of which yield the boundary empirically.
The algorithm is matched to each environment's learnability: where coordination
is learnable by independent agents we use PPO, and where the coordination reward
is shared-global we use a centralised critic, so that the measured boundary
reflects the behavioural incentive $w$ rather than the learner's capacity to
assign credit.

\paragraph{Supply Chain (NetworkManagement-v1, logistics/operations research).}
Six nodes in a serial supply chain share a common upstream supplier with
finite Poisson-demand ($\mu=20$).  The locally rational action is to
\emph{order aggressively}---maximising one's own on-hand inventory---which
depletes the shared upstream pipeline and starves downstream nodes.
At $w{=}0$ (pure individual inventory reward), agents hoard; the cascade failure
of downstream nodes constitutes collapse.
This is the primary experimental setting; results are reported in
Section~\ref{sec:results}.

\paragraph{MPE Cooperative Navigation (simple\_spread\_v3, robotics/navigation).}
Three agents must cover three landmarks in a continuous 2-D arena (discrete
action space, 50-step episodes).
The locally rational action under the herding incentive is to
\emph{stay near teammates}---individually rational for proximity bonuses---but
collectively disastrous, as all agents cluster and no landmark is covered.
The $w$-dial interpolates between the herding signal ($w{=}0$, clustering collapse)
and the global coverage score ($w{=}1$, coordination).
We use $\texttt{local\_ratio}{=}0$ in \texttt{simple\_spread\_v3} to ensure the
raw environment reward is the pure global team score; the herding term is defined
by our wrapper.
Collapse is measured by \emph{per-step landmark occupancy} (the fraction of
landmarks within a fixed radius of some agent), a behavioural metric that is
invariant to the reward scale and therefore unaffected by the CPT transform.
Crucially, unlike the supply chain---whose incentives are \emph{per-agent}, so
that collapse is purely behavioural---MPE's coordination reward is a
\emph{shared, global} team score. Under independent learners this confounds
behavioural collapse with a credit-assignment failure: with a decentralised
critic the agents receive an identical learning signal and cannot learn which
agent should cover which landmark, so the $w{=}1$ coordination end never trains
regardless of the behavioural incentive. We therefore train MPE with
\emph{centralised-critic MAPPO}~\citep{yu2022surprising}: each agent's actor
consumes only its local observation (augmented with a one-hot agent identifier
to break the symmetry of parameter sharing), while the critic receives the joint
observation. This is centralised training with decentralised execution; it
isolates the behavioural collapse boundary from the algorithm's ability to assign
credit---precisely the confound the reward-transform design is intended to avoid.
MPE is a standard benchmark introduced by \citet{lowe2017multi}.

\paragraph{Structural commonality.}
In both environments the incentive misalignment is identical at the level of the
reward blend:
\begin{equation}
  r_{\text{blend}}(i) = (1-w)\,r_{\text{local}}(i) \;+\; w\,r_{\text{global}},
\end{equation}
where $r_{\text{local}}$ encodes a locally rational strategy that is harmful to
the collective, and $r_{\text{global}}$ is the team score. The supply chain
exhibits a \emph{resource-competition} failure mode (hoarding stock starves
downstream nodes), while MPE exhibits a \emph{coordination-failure} mode (herding
prevents coverage)---structurally distinct routes to the same collapse boundary.
The collapse boundary is recovered in both environments we measure---the supply
chain (PPO) and MPE (MAPPO). To make MPE a fair test of the behavioural
decomposition rather than of the algorithm's capacity to coordinate, we set the
herding scale so the null boundary lands in the interior ($\wcrit{=}0.74$, rather
than pinned against the centralization ceiling) and ran the full loss-aversion
sweep $\lambda\in\{1,\ldots,7\}$ at $\alpha{=}\beta{=}0.88$ (1{,}760 runs across
the decomposition and $\lambda$-fill sweeps, 20 seeds per cell;
cross-setting summary in \Cref{fig:generalization}, per-$w$ curves in
App.~\ref{sec:appendix_genfigs}).

The \emph{loss-aversion} effect replicates. On MPE (full $\lambda{=}1{\ldots}7$
sweep, 20 seeds per cell), $\wcrit$ rises with loss aversion from $0.78$
($\lambda{=}1$) to $0.89$ ($\lambda{=}7$)---rising in envelope but non-monotone at
intermediate $\lambda$ (the $\lambda{=}2,4$ points dip to within seed noise of the
null), so the effect is read as a direction, not a strict ordering. The
loss-aversion excess over the null is $+0.158$ $[+0.108, +0.212]$ at
$\lambda{=}7$ and $+0.101$ $[+0.055, +0.150]$ at $\lambda{=}3$, both significant
and the latter quantitatively matching the supply-chain $+0.089$. The
qualitative law---loss aversion raises the centralization required to avert
collapse---thus holds across structurally distinct environments and training
algorithms (a third environment, Level-Based Foraging, is added in
Section~\ref{sec:lbf}; \Cref{fig:generalization}).

The \emph{curvature} effect does \emph{not} replicate. On MPE the curvature
excess is $+0.042$ $[-0.009, +0.092]$, statistically indistinguishable from the
null and opposite in sign to the supply-chain $-0.100$; probability weighting
appears to facilitate coordination only in the supply-chain substrate. The exact
\emph{functional form} of the loss-aversion law is likewise environment-specific:
the affine $\wcrit(\lambda)$ that tracks the supply chain to RMSE $0.014$ fits MPE
only to RMSE $0.05$, the MPE curve being steeper at high $\lambda$ as the boundary
approaches the centralization ceiling. We therefore claim cross-environment
generality for the collapse boundary and for the \emph{direction} of the
loss-aversion effect---the paper's central behavioural claim---while reporting the
curvature sign and the precise functional form as supply-chain-specific.


\subsection{A third environment: Level-Based Foraging}
\label{sec:lbf}
MPE shares MAPPO's shared-global reward; to probe a third, structurally distinct
domain we add Level-Based Foraging (LBF), a standard cooperative-MARL
benchmark~\citep{christianos2020shared} with a \emph{public-goods} incentive:
agents pay an effort cost to load food cooperatively, so the locally rational
action is to free-ride and the team coordinates only as the global reward comes
to dominate. LBF is natively discrete and unrelated to logistics or navigation;
we train it under centralized-critic MAPPO---matching MPE, so that across MPE and
LBF the \emph{algorithm} is held fixed and only the \emph{environment}
varies---with the identical reward pipeline and full $\lambda$-sweep, and a
collapse threshold set by the same collapsed-vs-coordinated midpoint used for MPE
(1{,}600 runs, $w\in\{0,\ldots,0.95\}$ including the $w{=}0.90$ resolver).

LBF yields the most complete decomposition outside the supply chain
(\Cref{fig:generalization}). The risk-neutral null has a low boundary
($\wcrit\approx0.08$); curvature moves it \emph{right} by $+0.18$---\emph{opposite}
to the supply chain's $-0.10$, again showing the curvature sign is
substrate-specific. Loss aversion then drives a large rightward shift: $\wcrit$
rises from $0.26$ ($\lambda{=}1$) to $0.83$ ($\lambda{=}4$), monotone with
non-overlapping low- versus high-$\lambda$ intervals (the pre-registered
criterion). For $\lambda{\geq}5$ the boundary saturates against the centralization
ceiling and we report those points as exploratory (a $w{=}0.90$ resolver sharpens
the mid-$\lambda$ estimates but does not change this). As in MPE, the
\emph{direction} replicates while curvature sign and functional form do not: the
affine law misfits LBF by RMSE $0.15$ (versus $0.014$ on the supply chain),
because the LBF boundary rises more steeply in $\lambda$. The \emph{method}---a
CI'd boundary that loss aversion shifts---generalizes; the affine \emph{constants
and form} are substrate-specific.

\subsection{Across algorithms: MAPPO on the supply chain}
\label{sec:appendix_crossalgo}

The MPE result varies the environment \emph{and} the algorithm at once. To
isolate the algorithm, we re-run the full loss-aversion sweep on the
\emph{identical} supply-chain substrate, grid, seeds, collapse label, and
hyperparameters, changing only the learner from independent PPO to
centralized-critic MAPPO~\citep{yu2022surprising} (700 runs;
$\lambda\in\{1,\ldots,7\}$, $\alpha{=}\beta{=}0.88$). The loss-aversion law
replicates: $\wcrit$ rises with loss aversion from $0.796$ $[0.742, 0.860]$ at
$\lambda{=}1$ to $0.889$ $[0.856, 0.917]$ at $\lambda{=}7$, monotone from
$\lambda{=}2$ on, with non-overlapping $\lambda{=}1$ vs $\lambda{=}7$ intervals
(\Cref{fig:generalization}; per-$w$ curves in
App.~\ref{sec:appendix_genfigs}). As in MPE, the \emph{direction} generalizes
while the absolute level does not---the MAPPO boundary sits above PPO at low
$\lambda$ (where its transition is shallow and noisy) and the two converge by
$\lambda{\geq}4$ ($|\Delta\wcrit|\leq 0.04$)---so we report the direction and
monotonicity as algorithm-independent, and the absolute $\wcrit$ as
algorithm-dependent.


% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
% [Ablations and the heterogeneous-minority result relocated to appendix
%  for AAAI body length; see App.~\ref{sec:appendix_ablations} and
%  App.~\ref{sec:appendix_heterogeneous}. Body keeps pointers only.]
Two ablations confirm the boundary is contingent on the ingredients the
mechanism names: it disappears under coarse discretisation of the continuous
control (removing the policy gradient) and is measurable only with a
credit-assignment-capable learner on shared-reward tasks
(App.~\ref{sec:appendix_ablations}). Finally, the boundary is not an averaged
property: a single \emph{downstream} loss-averse node can set the centralization
requirement of the whole network, a cascade that propagates upstream along the
ordering chain (App.~\ref{sec:appendix_heterogeneous}; discussed below).
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

% D1: the opposite-effects finding is the main result
That probability weighting and loss aversion exert opposite effects on the
coordination boundary is, to our knowledge, novel; prior work applies prospect
theory as a unified model without decomposing its
components~\citep{lalmohammed2025,ghaemi2024risk}. Our null-model design isolates
them: on the supply chain the beneficial curvature effect ($-0.100$,
substrate-specific) is masked when combined with loss aversion, whose
centralization-raising effect dominates and is the component we establish as
general. The operational reading is concrete: a system with $\lambda{=}7$
agents---bracketing human loss-aversion estimates~\citep{tversky1992}---requires
$w>0.851$ for reliable coordination against $w>0.685$ for risk-neutral agents,
so the additional $0.166$ (\Cref{tab:decomposition}) is a quantified cost of
deploying loss-averse agents.

% D1c: calibration of lambda + practical constraints on centralization
Two caveats sharpen this reading. On calibration, the canonical laboratory
estimate of loss aversion is $\lambda\approx2.25$~\citep{tversky1992}; our
sweep deliberately brackets the empirical human range, with $\lambda{=}7$ an
upper, high-stakes bound rather than a typical value. The cost scales with
$\lambda$ (\Cref{tab:decomposition}), so a domain whose participants sit nearer
$\lambda\approx2$--$3$ incurs a smaller but still positive margin (the
$\lambda{=}3$ excess is $+0.089$, against $+0.166$ at $\lambda{=}7$). On
deployment, centralization is not free: privacy, communication cost, and
organisational policy can cap the attainable $w$. Where the required $w$
exceeds that cap, the boundary reframes the design problem---instead of only
raising centralization, one can lower the agents' effective loss aversion
(for instance through the training objective), treating $\lambda$ as the
second, behavioural lever the decomposition exposes.

% D1b: heterogeneous minority -- full treatment promoted to its own section
The boundary is also not an averaged property. App.~\ref{sec:appendix_heterogeneous}
shows it is set by a \emph{minority}: a single downstream loss-averse node
moves the system boundary as far as a fully loss-averse population, cascading
upstream along the ordering chain. The operational consequence is sharp---in a
mixed human--agent supply chain, the required centralization is governed by the
most downstream loss-averse participant, not by the population mean.

% D2: metastability -- lazy agent attractor is escapable
The sliding $\wcrit$ is a positive finding, not a pathology: the lazy-agent
attractor is \emph{metastable}, so agents caught below the isocline at finite
$T$ can in principle coordinate with more training---consistent with
\citet{weng2026}, where exploration breaks cooperation in shared-policy MARL
before representations converge. The practical implication is that the minimum
$w$ depends on the training budget: tighter horizons demand higher
centralization.

% D3: relation to Yamaguchi 2025
\citet{yamaguchi2025} construct an analogous $(L,\rho)$ phase diagram for IQL;
our results complement theirs by showing the phase structure depends not only on
environmental complexity but on reward structure ($w$) and behavioral parameters
($\lambda$). More broadly, competing-bias tipping points are an established analytic
phenomenon in multiagent dynamics: \citet{capannoli2026phase} prove a metastable
partial-adoption boundary in network opinion dynamics under competing stubbornness
and disruptive biases, with an explicit critical threshold. Our contribution is
the deep-MARL \emph{learning-dynamics} analogue---a boundary set by the
policy-gradient signal-to-noise ratio rather than a fixed update rule---which
situates our phase-diagram framing within a recognised line of multiagent
phase-transition results.

% D4: limitations
Several limitations bound these claims. \emph{Substrate scope:} the full
quantitative characterization (decomposition, affine $\lambda$-law, metastability,
cascade) uses one substrate, chosen because its continuous, per-agent economic
reward makes $\wcrit$ finely identifiable; we replicate the boundary and the
loss-aversion \emph{direction} in two further environments (MPE, LBF) and under a
second algorithm on the same substrate, but the curvature sign and the exact
functional form do \emph{not} transfer, so we claim only the loss-aversion
direction as cross-substrate. \emph{Sweep scope:} the steep high-$\lambda$
transitions are resolved by a single interior grid point (handled with Firth
fits, \Cref{tab:lambda_sweep}); the metastability result is finite-horizon; and
the heterogeneity sweep uses binary $\lambda\in\{1,7\}$. \emph{Method scope:} the
approach needs an environment learnable without experience sharing and a collapse
label separable from task-unlearnability, excluding sparse, long-credit-horizon
tasks. \emph{Cascade scope:} the downstream cascade is single-substrate by
structural necessity---it is well-posed only where a directed resource-flow
ordering, genuine local-vs-global tension, and a learning-reachable boundary
co-occur, a rare conjunction most benchmarks violate
(App.~\ref{sec:appendix_heterogeneous}); we therefore frame it as a falsifiable,
structurally specified prediction rather than an established generality.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first quantitative phase diagram of cooperative MARL
coordination collapse, characterising the critical centralization threshold
$\wcrit$ as a function of reward structure, training horizon, and
prospect-theoretic loss aversion.
Our central and most strongly supported finding is that \emph{loss aversion}
impedes coordination---raising $\wcrit$ ($+0.089$ at $\lambda{=}3$, within the
human range; up to $+0.166$ at $\lambda{=}7$)---a direction we predict
mechanistically a priori and confirm across three environments and two
algorithms. Scoped to the supply-chain substrate, two further results sharpen
the picture: a three-way decomposition shows probability-weighting curvature
acting in the \emph{opposite} direction (easing coordination by $0.100$), and
the boundary is set by a downstream loss-averse \emph{minority} rather than the
mean bias. We present the latter as a striking single-substrate result and frame
it as a falsifiable prediction for any directed-flow domain, rather than a
claim shown to generalize.
These results provide concrete design constraints for deploying cooperative
MARL in behavioral settings and establish a pre-registered phase-diagram
methodology applicable to any cooperative MARL system.

Natural extensions include replication across further algorithms (QMIX) and
environments (SMAC), upgrading the mean-field account
(Section~\ref{sec:meanfield}) from a calibrated mechanism to a quantitative
prediction of $\wcrit$ via direct measurement of the policy-gradient
signal-to-noise ratio (Section~\ref{sec:appendix_snr}), adaptive centralization
scheduling based on the estimated isocline, and characterization of
heterogeneous-$\lambda$ populations.

