\section{Boundary Conditions: Two Ablations}
\label{sec:appendix_ablations}

The mean-field account (Section~\ref{sec:meanfield}) makes the boundary contingent on
two ingredients: a \emph{continuous} policy gradient (the signal-to-noise
crossing is a property of gradient flow), and a learner able to separate
behavioural collapse from credit assignment (so that what $w$ moves is the
incentive, not the optimiser's capacity to coordinate). We ablate each. Both
confirm the mechanism and delimit \emph{where} the phenomenon lives.

\subsection{Action representation}
\label{sec:ablation_action}
The mechanism predicts that destroying the continuous policy gradient should
remove the boundary. Value-based learners require a discrete action space, so we
discretised the supply-chain base-stock control into $K{=}11$ bins and ran an
independent value learner (IQL) and a centralised monotonic-mixing learner
(QMIX) on the identical reward pipeline, $w$-grid, and collapse label. Both
produced a \emph{flat, $w$-independent} collapse rate with no fittable $\wcrit$
in any condition. To isolate the cause---value-based learning vs.\ the discrete
action space---we add a single-variable control: PPO, the learner that yields
the boundary under continuous control, trained on the \emph{same} $K{=}11$
discrete shell with everything else fixed. It too goes flat
(\Cref{tab:ablation_action}). Holding the learner at PPO, discretisation
\emph{alone} erases the boundary, so the value-based nulls require no appeal to
the learning paradigm.

We scope this precisely. The supply-chain action is a \emph{continuous}
base-stock level; coarse bins snap the policy to a stable interior level and
never visit the near-threshold $(s,S)$ region where collapse occurs, exactly as
the continuous-gradient mechanism predicts. This is \emph{not} a claim that
discreteness per se removes the boundary: MPE (Section~\ref{sec:generalization}) has a
\emph{natively} discrete action space and retains it. The effect is therefore a
coarse-discretisation boundary condition, substrate-specific in the same way the
curvature sign is---and a confirmed prediction of the mechanism, not a negative
result.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering \small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Learner & Action space & Boundary? \\
\midrule
PPO / MAPPO              & continuous        & yes (Section~\ref{sec:results}) \\
QMIX (centralised value) & discrete $K{=}11$ & no \\
IQL (independent value)  & discrete $K{=}11$ & no \\
PPO (control)            & discrete $K{=}11$ & \textbf{no} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Action-representation ablation. The control (last row) changes only the
action space while holding the learner at PPO, isolating coarse discretisation of
the continuous control---not the value-based paradigm---as what erases the
boundary.}
\label{tab:ablation_action}
\end{table}

\subsection{Credit assignment}
\label{sec:ablation_credit}
The second ingredient is a learner that can coordinate at all on a shared-reward
task, so that $\wcrit$ reflects the behavioural incentive rather than a
credit-assignment failure. We ablate the centralised critic. On MPE Cooperative
Navigation, an independent learner (PPO) never reaches the coordinated regime at
\emph{any} $w$: the collapse fraction is $1.0$ across the entire $w$-range,
including maximal centralisation (\Cref{fig:mpe_floor}), so behavioural collapse
cannot be distinguished from the inability to assign credit. A centralised critic
(MAPPO) on the identical environment recovers coordination---the collapse
fraction falls to $0.35$ and landmark occupancy crosses the calibrated threshold
near $w{\approx}0.6$. We observe the same independent-learner floor on
Level-Based Foraging, where PPO fails to coordinate while MAPPO reaches the
coordinated regime. The boundary is therefore \emph{measurable} only with a
centralised critic on shared-reward coordination tasks; this is why we match a
centralised critic to such environments (Section~\ref{sec:generalization})---a
necessity, not a convenience.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.82\linewidth]{../docs/expPPO_mpe_figures/mpe_ppo_coordination_floor.png}
  \caption{Credit-assignment ablation on MPE (null, $\lambda{=}1$). Independent
  PPO collapses at every $w$ (a coordination floor: it cannot learn the task
  regardless of incentive), while the centralised-critic learner (MAPPO) crosses
  the boundary. The behavioural boundary is exposed only once credit assignment
  is handled.}
  \label{fig:mpe_floor}
\end{figure}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{A Minority Sets the Boundary (Single-Substrate Result)}
\label{sec:appendix_heterogeneous}

The decomposition and its generalization treat the population as
homogeneous---every agent carries the same $\lambda$. A deployed cooperative
system rarely is: some participants are loss-averse, others are not. We
therefore ask whether the boundary is an averaged, population-wide property,
or whether a \emph{minority} can set it---and whether \emph{network position}
matters. We assign $\lambda{=}7$ to selected nodes and $\lambda{=}1$ to the
rest, holding the substrate, PPO configuration, collapse label, and grid
identical to the main sweep ($\alpha{=}\beta{=}0.88$, 300k, 20 seeds,
$w\in\{0.5,0.65,0.75,0.85,0.95\}$).

The effect is large and strongly position-dependent (\Cref{fig:hetero},
\Cref{tab:hetero}). A single \emph{downstream} loss-averse node drives
$\wcrit = 0.853$ $[0.849,0.858]$---statistically indistinguishable from the
\emph{fully} loss-averse population ($\wcrit = 0.851$ $[0.847,0.856]$): one
biased node downstream sets the centralization requirement of the entire
network. The same node placed \emph{upstream} shifts the boundary far less,
to $\wcrit = 0.726$ $[0.689,0.763]$. Loss aversion therefore cascades
\emph{upstream}---a downstream node's bias propagates back along the ordering
chain and dominates the system boundary---but not downstream, a directional
asymmetry that mirrors the bullwhip structure of the substrate.

That a behavioral minority can move a system-level tipping point is itself
established in evolutionary game theory via replicator
selection~\citep{mengesha2025}; our contribution is the deep-MARL
instantiation, where the effect emerges from gradient-learning dynamics and
carries a concrete network-position signature absent from the EGT account.
The operational reading is sharper than a population average would suggest:
in a mixed human--agent supply chain, the centralization the system needs is
set by its most downstream loss-averse participant, not by the mean bias.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Heterogeneous-population sweep ($\alpha{=}\beta{=}0.88$; loss-averse
nodes at $\lambda{=}7$, others $\lambda{=}1$; 300k, 20 seeds/cell).
$\wcrit$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs. Homogeneous references: all risk-neutral
$\approx 0.586$, all loss-averse $0.851$.}
\label{tab:hetero}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Loss-averse nodes & $\wcrit$ (95\% CI) \\
\midrule
one upstream                   & 0.726 [0.689, 0.763] \\
one downstream                 & 0.853 [0.849, 0.858] \\
two downstream                 & 0.851 [0.847, 0.856] \\
all five (homogeneous)         & 0.851 [0.847, 0.856] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
